1. IaaS / PaaS / SaaS
- IaaS: Infrastructura as a Service. Renting raw compute infrastructure (servers, storage, networking) over the internet. Fully customisable across all layers, but you are responsible for managing the OS, runtime, and keeping everything up to date. Best for teams with technical expertise who need full control. (e.g. AWS EC2, Azure VMs)
- PaaS:Platform as a Service. The infrastructure and runtime environment are managed for you — you only focus on building and deploying your application. Customisable at the application layer, but the underlying OS and middleware are abstracted away. Best for developers who want to ship code without managing servers. (e.g. Heroku, Google App Engine)
- SaaS: Software as a service. A ready-to-use software solution delivered over the internet. Minimal customisation — you configure it, not build it. Best when a prebuilt solution fits your needs. (e.g. Salesforce, Slack, Gmail)

2. Shared Responsibility Model
| Layer | IaaS | PaaS | SaaS |
| Application | You | You | Provide |
| Runtime/Middleware | You | Provide | Provider |
| OS | You | Provide | Provider |
| Infrastructure | Provide | Provider | Provider |
Runtime: Execution environment your code needs to run. For instance: If you write an app in Python, you need Python installed 
Middleware: Software that acts as "glue" between your application and the OS, or between different components. For instance: A web server like Nginx or Apache OR an API gateway 

3. Public / Private / Hybrid cloud
- Public Cloud: Don't own servers, you rent them like AWS, GCP (Google Cloud Platform), Azure
- Private Cloud: You own servers in your premises. Key for compliance as Australia requires to keep data under certain standards and within Australia for certain industries
- Hybrid Cloud: A mix, when have a portion public (less important data) and Private (more secret data)

4. CapEx vs OpEx
CapEx: Capital Expediture: it's an upfront investment in long-term assets that depreciate over time. 
OpEx: Operational Expenditure: Cost of running an operation. These are recurring costs, expensed in the period they occur — no depreciation
| - | CapEx | OpEx | 
| Payment | Upfront, Large | Recurrent, Small |
| Accounting | Depreciated over time | Expensed Inmidiatly |
| Example | Buy Servers | VPS Hetzner |
| Flexibility | Low (Buy for highest peak of usage) | High (Pay as you need) |

5. Regiones y Availability Zones
Regiones: A specific geographic area (e.g. Australia East, US East) where a cloud provider has infrastructure. Chosen based on latency, compliance, and data sovereignty requirements.
Availability Zones:  One or more independent data centres within a region, each with separate power, cooling and networking. Designed so that a failure in one AZ doesn't affect the others.

6. AWS Vs Azure Vs GCP
AWS Pros: Largest in market, with more solutions than any other, better documentation, mature 
AWS Cons: Harder to learn due to the amount of solutions and expensive if not matching solutions and issues.
Azure Pros: Native Microsoft integration, Open AI GPT-4 in your own Azure tenant - your data not goint to internet keep it safe and private. Leading in Gob, education and traditional enterprise
Azure Cons: Not great for start-ups and tech businesses, slow launching new products, weak out of Microsoft
GCP Pros: Best stack when the problem is data + AI together. BigQuery (Process and analyze data) --> AI Training --> Vertex AI (Build and train models) --> Gemini (LLM queries), better price 
GCP Cons: Smaller presence, less products

7.containers and orchestration
VM:  It's a computer living in a cloud, with own OS and kernel (software that talkd directly with hardware)
Container: A container virtualizes only the execution environment — it shares the host's kernel but has its own filesystem (directory structure independt and hidden from other container), environment variables (Each container has its own set. That's how your docker-compose.yml passes the database password to n8n without writing it inside the app itself.), and network (By default a container can't talk to other containers or to the internet unless you explicitly allow it). Much lighter than a VM.
Volume: A folder on the host (VPS) mounted inside a container. Data persists independently of the container being erase.
Secrets: A mechanism to securely deliver sensitive values (passwords, API keys, encryption keys) to an application at runtime — without storing them in plain text in config files.
Image: Template, read-only. Once this is set with a specific configuration and data is called a Container
  Registry  (Docker Hub) : Server with all repositories (Docker Hub / AWS ECR)
      └── Repository  (n8nio/n8n): The collection of all versions of an image
              └── Image/Tags/versions  (n8nio/n8n:latest, n8nio/n8n:1.0.0): an specific version of the repository
Docker Compose: Your /opt/n8n/docker-compose.yml file defines your entire stack: n8n, PostgreSQL, volumes, networks, and environment variables — all in a single file.
Docker Compose is a file that defines your entire stack — images, configuration, volumes, networks, and dependencies — so you can spin up or recreate your environment with a single command.
  └──  docker compose up -d
 docker-compose.yml
      ├── Which images to use (and which version)
      ├── How to configure each container (environment variables)
      ├── Which volumes to mount (where data persists)
      ├── How they communicate with each other (networks)
      └── In which order they start (dependencies)
